      body: 'The vrat is incomplete without its two **kathas**. The first is **Jimutavahana** — the compassionate prince (hero of Harsha’s 7th-century play *Nagananda*) who, finding a Naga mother whose son **Shankhachuda** is owed to the eagle **Garuda**, lies on the sacrifice-rock in the youth’s place; Garuda, moved by his selflessness, vows to stop devouring serpents — and from that protective compassion the fast for children’s long life is said to flow.\n\nThe second is the **Chilho-Siyarin** (eagle and jackal) tale: both she-creatures vow to keep Jitiya, but the jackal breaks the fast while the eagle holds firm. Reborn as sisters, the eagle’s children thrive and the jackal’s are lost — until faithful observance restores them. It teaches that the vow must be kept faultlessly.',
